<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_inheritance_tradeoffs_and_design_choices">
    <title>Inheritance Trade-Offs and Design Choices</title>

    <p>
        Inheritance can express a genuine is-a relationship, but it is not simply a
        general-purpose mechanism for reusing code. A derived class becomes coupled
        to the base class's interface and behavior. Before choosing inheritance, we
        should ask whether the derived object can really be used wherever the base
        object is expected.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_inheritance_coupling">
        <title>Inheritance Creates Coupling</title>

        <p>
            A derived class depends on details of its base class. Changes to the base
            class can therefore affect every derived class. A short hierarchy may be
            easy to understand, but a deep hierarchy can make it difficult to locate
            where behavior comes from.
        </p>

        <p>
            Inheritance can also expose behavior that does not make sense for the
            derived type. If a derived class must disable, reinterpret, or contradict
            an inherited operation, the proposed is-a relationship may be incorrect.
            Sharing a few lines of implementation is not enough to justify
            inheritance.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_inheritance_tradeoffs">
            <title>Common inheritance trade-offs</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="27%"/>
                <col width="48%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Concern</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>What can happen</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Design question</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Tight coupling</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Base-class changes affect derived classes.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Could the behavior be provided by a component instead?</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Deep hierarchy</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Behavior becomes difficult to trace.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Can the design remain shallow and easy to explain?</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Wrong is-a relationship</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The derived class inherits unsuitable operations.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Can every derived object substitute for the base object?</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Implementation reuse only</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The hierarchy communicates a relationship that is not real.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Would composition or a helper function express the goal better?</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_classes_not_designed_for_inheritance">
        <title>Not Every Class Is Designed for Inheritance</title>

        <p>
            C++ does not forbid inheriting from every standard-library class, but a
            class such as <c>std::string</c> is not generally designed to be a
            polymorphic base class. It has no virtual destructor for safe deletion
            through a <c>std::string*</c>, and it does not provide a virtual interface
            intended for derived customization.
        </p>

        <p>
            A wrapper class uses composition to add a label while keeping the
            original string as a private component:
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_labeled_text_wrapper">
            <title>A wrapper adds behavior through composition</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_labeled_text_wrapper"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class LabeledText {
private:
    std::string text;
    std::string label;

public:
    LabeledText(const std::string& starting_label,
                const std::string& starting_text)
        : text{starting_text}, label{starting_label} {
    }

    void print() const {
        std::cout << label << ": " << text << '\n';
    }

    std::size_t length() const {
        return text.length();
    }
};

int main() {
    LabeledText message{"Status", "Ready"};
    message.print();
    std::cout << message.length() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>LabeledText</c> is not a specialized kind of <c>std::string</c>. It
            has a string and provides only the operations that belong in its own
            interface. This wrapper design avoids making a standard-library class
            part of an unnecessary inheritance hierarchy.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_prefer_composition_guidance">
        <title>When Composition Is the Better Choice</title>

        <p>
            Composition is often a better choice when the goal is to combine
            implementation components, replace one component with another, or keep
            class responsibilities independent. Inheritance is more appropriate when
            a derived object must genuinely satisfy the base type's public contract.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_choose_composition_or_inheritance">
            <title>Choosing between composition and inheritance</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="32%"/>
                <col width="34%"/>
                <col width="34%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Question</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Composition is likely when...</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Inheritance is likely when...</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>What is the relationship?</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The object has or uses another object.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>The object is a specialized version of the base type.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>What should change?</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A component may be replaced independently.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Derived types share a stable public contract.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Why reuse behavior?</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>To delegate implementation work.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>To support substitutability and shared polymorphic behavior.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            <q>Prefer composition over inheritance</q> is useful guidance, not an
            absolute rule. The important step is to choose the relationship that
            accurately communicates the design.
        </p>

        <p>
            The visualization shows a wrapper object containing a
            <c>std::string</c> rather than inheriting from it.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_wrapper_composition"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%23include%20%3Cstring%3E%0A%0Aclass%20LabeledText%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Astring%20text%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Astring%20label%3B%0A%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20LabeledText%28const%20std%3A%3Astring%26%20starting_label%2C%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20const%20std%3A%3Astring%26%20starting_text%29%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%3A%20text%7Bstarting_text%7D%2C%20label%7Bstarting_label%7D%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20print%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20label%20%3C%3C%20%22%3A%20%22%20%3C%3C%20text%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Asize_t%20length%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20return%20text.length%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20LabeledText%20message%7B%22Status%22%2C%20%22Ready%22%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20message.print%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20message.length%28%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_inheritance_tradeoffs">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_tradeoffs_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which design is usually better for adding a label to a
                    <c>std::string</c>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Always inherit publicly from <c>std::string</c>.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>std::string</c> is not generally designed as a polymorphic base class.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Use a wrapper class that contains a <c>std::string</c>.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition keeps the string as an implementation component.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Make the label a global variable.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>A global variable does not model the relationship between one label and one string.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Copy the string into every function that prints it.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Repeated copying does not create a clear object design.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_tradeoffs_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which situations favor composition over inheritance? Select all
                    that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>The object has another object as one of its parts.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This is a has-a relationship.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A component may need to be replaced independently.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition can reduce dependence on a fixed class hierarchy.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>The derived object must be usable wherever the base type is expected.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This is a reason to consider inheritance and polymorphism.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>The design goal is to delegate implementation work to another object.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Delegation is a common composition technique.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_tradeoffs_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why is sharing a few lines of implementation not, by itself, a
                    sufficient reason to use inheritance?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Inheritance communicates an is-a relationship and creates
                    coupling between the base and derived classes. Composition or a
                    helper function may reuse implementation without making a false
                    type relationship.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_tradeoffs_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to define a wrapper class that contains a
                    <c>std::string</c>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class LabeledText {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>private:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    std::string text;</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>    void print() const {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="6">
                    <cline>        std::cout &lt;&lt; text &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="7">
                    <cline>    }</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="8">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="1" correct="no">
                    <cline>class LabeledText : public std::string {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
